1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.020
[S01] Have you ever felt like life was passing right in front of your eyes and you weren't

2
00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.250
[S01] doing a thing about it?

3
00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:08.710
[S01] Waking up late, scrolling on your phone, playing one more game, watching one more

4
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.820
[S01] video, telling yourself, "I'll start in a little bit," but that little bit never comes?

5
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.179
[S01] And by the end of the day, you're lying there again with that same familiar question,

6
00:00:18.179 --> 00:00:20.410
[S01] "What the heck am I doing with my life?"

7
00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.109
[S01] Most people call this laziness.

8
00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:25.019
[S01] They'll tell you to be more disciplined, to manage your time better.

9
00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:28.579
[S01] But the truth is, your problem has nothing to do with time management skills.

10
00:00:28.579 --> 00:00:31.019
[S01] What's holding you back is not laziness.

11
00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:34.539
[S01] It's a very deep part of your mind quietly avoiding growing up.

12
00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:36.109
[S01] And what's the scariest part?

13
00:00:36.109 --> 00:00:39.759
[S01] A lot of men don't grow out of this in their twenties.

14
00:00:39.759 --> 00:00:44.780
[S01] They carry it into their thirties, forties, fifties, even their sixties.

15
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.049
[S01] On the outside, they still look like adults.

16
00:00:47.049 --> 00:00:52.119
[S01] But on the inside, there's a very childish part of them that panics every time life

17
00:00:52.119 --> 00:00:54.280
[S01] asks them to take responsibility.

18
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.670
[S01] Today, we're going to step into the psychology of the man-child.

19
00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.460
[S01] Before we begin, I need to say this upfront.

20
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.009
[S01] This video may not be easy to hear.

21
00:01:03.009 --> 00:01:07.480
[S01] It might make you uncomfortable, maybe even hit your ego a little.

22
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.750
[S01] But if you can stay calm enough to listen until the end, this might be the exact wake-up

23
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.920
[S01] call you need to truly snap out of it.

24
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.219
[S01] So what exactly is this childish part?

25
00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:20.060
[S01] And what form is it hiding inside you?

26
00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.859
[S01] Let's start by dissecting its psychology.

27
00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:29.430
[S01] The psychoanalyst Carl Jung used a Latin term, "poor eternus," to describe this state.

28
00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.819
[S01] It means "the eternal boy."

29
00:01:31.819 --> 00:01:37.750
[S01] In everyday life, we know it by names like Peter Pan Syndrome or "the man-child."

30
00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:41.609
[S01] When you are caught in this syndrome, it does not mean you are unintelligent or incapable.

31
00:01:41.609 --> 00:01:45.670
[S01] In fact, you are often someone with a lot of ideas and sharp instincts.

32
00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.390
[S01] However, your biggest trait in this state is an extreme desire for absolute freedom.

33
00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:55.750
[S01] In this state, freedom seems to mean not being tied down by anything, not being responsible

34
00:01:55.750 --> 00:02:00.030
[S01] for anyone, and not having to make any fixed choices.

35
00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:04.450
[S01] You want to keep yourself in a place where every door stays open, but you never actually

36
00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.140
[S01] walk through any of them.

37
00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:11.340
[S01] Carl Jung described your life with a very accurate phrase: "the provisional life."

38
00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.129
[S01] Living a provisional life means you always feel like what you are doing right now is

39
00:02:15.129 --> 00:02:16.939
[S01] not your real life.

40
00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:22.159
[S01] You work an office job, but you tell yourself it is only a temporary job to make a living,

41
00:02:22.159 --> 00:02:26.810
[S01] while deep down, you are actually an undiscovered genius waiting for your moment.

42
00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.439
[S01] You live in a messy room, but you think there is no need to clean it because this is only

43
00:02:30.439 --> 00:02:33.689
[S01] a temporary place, and one day, you will have a real home.

44
00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:38.270
[S02] You date someone, but deep inside, you always think this is not the most perfect

45
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:41.729
[S02] person for you, and you are only in a temporary relationship.

46
00:02:41.729 --> 00:02:45.379
[S02] Because you think everything is temporary, you never put your full heart and effort into

47
00:02:45.379 --> 00:02:46.259
[S02] anything.

48
00:02:46.259 --> 00:02:50.669
[S02] You live like a spectator sitting in the stands, watching your own life pass by.

49
00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:53.939
[S02] You watch the people around you throw themselves into life.

50
00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:57.689
[S02] They work, they fail, they move up, they get married.

51
00:02:57.689 --> 00:03:02.389
[S02] Your mind looks at them and unconsciously creates the feeling that you are somehow

52
00:03:02.389 --> 00:03:03.569
[S02] above them.

53
00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:08.530
[S02] The ego of the child inside you tells itself that they are trapped in the rat race of

54
00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:12.919
[S02] bills and daily survival, while you are just waiting for a more special path.

55
00:03:12.919 --> 00:03:18.030
[S02] But the truth is, you choose to be a spectator, not because you are free, but because you

56
00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.569
[S02] are using that freedom as a shield.

57
00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:24.879
[S02] When you do not choose a specific career, you can proudly tell yourself that you have

58
00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:26.830
[S02] the potential to do anything.

59
00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:31.189
[S02] When you never truly give your all to any goal, you never have to face the feeling

60
00:03:31.189 --> 00:03:32.330
[S02] of failure.

61
00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:36.719
[S02] You keep all your dreams as perfect ideas in your head, because a dream that has never

62
00:03:36.719 --> 00:03:39.819
[S02] been tested, has never had the chance to fail.

63
00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:44.180
[S02] The deepest hidden fear behind your careless attitude is the fear of reality.

64
00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.710
[S02] Everyday reality always comes with harsh rules.

65
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:51.210
[S02] Reality has bills to pay, rejection, competition, and flaws.

66
00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:54.069
[S02] In that safe imaginary world, you are a star.

67
00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:58.099
[S02] But when you step into reality, you have to accept that you are starting from zero.

68
00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:03.349
[S02] You have to learn, you have to work, and you have to take responsibility for your mistakes.

69
00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:08.020
[S02] A part of you would rather cling to a safe provisional life, than step onto the field

70
00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.979
[S02] and accept the risk of getting hurt.

71
00:04:09.979 --> 00:04:14.740
[S02] So where did this chronic procrastination and fear of growing up actually come from?

72
00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:18.740
[S02] Why do you choose to trap yourself inside the cocoon of childhood, instead of stepping

73
00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.100
[S02] out into the world?

74
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.490
[S02] To answer that, we need to flip back through the pages of the past.

75
00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:29.269
[S02] Marie-Louise von Franz, a close colleague of Carl Jung, pointed out a surprising truth:

76
00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:33.740
[S02] Most man children, when they were young, were actually very gifted kids.

77
00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:37.379
[S02] Maybe as a child, you were the smartest kid in class, someone who learned everything

78
00:04:37.379 --> 00:04:41.850
[S02] quickly without much effort, or someone with a special talent in a certain area.

79
00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.589
[S02] You received tons of praise from your parents and teachers.

80
00:04:44.589 --> 00:04:48.149
[S02] You were labeled as a special child, or a future genius.

81
00:04:48.149 --> 00:04:53.180
[S02] These early compliments accidentally created a dangerous psychological trap.

82
00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.930
[S02] You began to build your entire sense of self-worth around being outstanding and full of great

83
00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.019
[S02] potential.

84
00:04:59.019 --> 00:05:01.519
[S02] You believed you were different from the rest of the world.

85
00:05:01.519 --> 00:05:06.870
[S02] However the tragedy begins when you enter adulthood and have to face real society.

86
00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:10.970
[S02] In the school of life, potential is no longer something that gets rewarded.

87
00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:15.060
[S02] The real world only recognizes results and persistence.

88
00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.079
[S02] When you start a new job or a new project, you cannot be great right away.

89
00:05:19.079 --> 00:05:22.800
[S02] You will make mistakes, you will be rejected, and you will see that there are many other

90
00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.300
[S02] people who are better than you.

91
00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.079
[S02] For someone who grew up believing they were special, these experiences feel like a direct

92
00:05:28.079 --> 00:05:30.019
[S02] threat to your ego.

93
00:05:30.019 --> 00:05:34.720
[S02] The feeling that you are not good enough, the feeling of being rejected, hurts deeply.

94
00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.750
[S02] And to protect that wounded ego, your mind activates an automatic defense mechanism.

95
00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.490
[S02] That mechanism is procrastination.

96
00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:47.300
[S02] You start telling yourself, "I have not succeeded only because I have not truly started taking

97
00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:50.970
[S02] things seriously yet, not because I am not good enough."

98
00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:56.970
[S02] When you delay applying for jobs, delay writing that book, delay learning a new skill, what

99
00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:00.769
[S02] you are really doing is protecting the illusion of your own ability.

100
00:06:00.769 --> 00:06:04.079
[S02] As long as you have not started, you still have the right to believe that if you did

101
00:06:04.079 --> 00:06:07.079
[S02] start, you would definitely become the best.

102
00:06:07.079 --> 00:06:10.939
[S02] At this point, procrastination is not simply laziness.

103
00:06:10.939 --> 00:06:16.189
[S02] It is a psychological strategy that helps you avoid facing your actual level of ability.

104
00:06:16.189 --> 00:06:18.720
[S02] Look at the story of Icarus in mythology.

105
00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.860
[S02] He was given a pair of wax wings to escape from the island where he was trapped, but

106
00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.079
[S02] he refused to respect the limits.

107
00:06:25.079 --> 00:06:30.329
[S02] He flew too high, too close to the sun, because he thought he was untouchable, until the

108
00:06:30.329 --> 00:06:33.889
[S02] heat melted the wax, and he fell into the sea.

109
00:06:33.889 --> 00:06:38.110
[S02] You are the image of Icarus when you confuse potential with real achievement.

110
00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:42.220
[S02] You can dream about flying very high in the wonderful plans you create for yourself.

111
00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:46.220
[S02] But, if you have not learned how to walk steadily on the ground, if you have not trained

112
00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:50.139
[S02] yourself through even the smallest tasks, you will be very easily shattered when you

113
00:06:50.139 --> 00:06:51.459
[S02] touch reality.

114
00:06:51.459 --> 00:06:56.540
[S02] And when the gap between fantasy and reality keeps getting bigger, people can easily fall

115
00:06:56.540 --> 00:07:01.139
[S02] into a self-destructive spiral with three familiar psychological traps of the eternal

116
00:07:01.139 --> 00:07:02.329
[S02] child.

117
00:07:02.329 --> 00:07:06.370
[S02] Trap one: Constantly looking for tools to numb your awareness.

118
00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:10.850
[S02] It is easy to understand why we want comfort after a tiring day, but the problem lies

119
00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:12.449
[S02] in how you use it.

120
00:07:12.449 --> 00:07:16.980
[S02] Every time you face the pressure of life, or the fear of failure, your familiar reaction

121
00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:21.620
[S02] is not to solve the problem, but to find a quick escape so you do not have to feel

122
00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:22.790
[S02] that discomfort.

123
00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.589
[S03] In the virtual world of a game, you can be a successful hero.

124
00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:47.509
[S03] But when you turn off the screen, you return to an empty reality.

125
00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:51.949
[S03] You are using these short-term pleasures to escape the alarm bell of your own life.

126
00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:56.019
[S03] Trap two: being extremely afraid of long-term commitment.

127
00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.699
[S03] In the early stage, when everything is still new and fun, you are very enthusiastic.

128
00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:05.350
[S03] But as soon as the relationship starts asking for responsibility, promises about the future,

129
00:08:05.379 --> 00:08:08.259
[S03] or financial sharing, you immediately feel trapped.

130
00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:13.079
[S03] You begin to exaggerate the other person's small flaws, focus on everything that is not

131
00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:18.470
[S03] perfect, or use your circumstances as a reasonable excuse to slowly pull away.

132
00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:22.230
[S03] On a deeper level, you may unconsciously be drawn to someone who takes care of you

133
00:08:22.259 --> 00:08:27.649
[S03] too much, and then slowly place on them the role of carrying your emotions and responsibilities

134
00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:28.540
[S03] for you.

135
00:08:28.569 --> 00:08:33.299
[S03] Over time, they have to carry too much, from cleaning and money to major decisions,

136
00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:37.649
[S03] while you stay stuck in the role of the person being taken care of, rather than someone

137
00:08:37.679 --> 00:08:39.389
[S03] who carries the weight with them.

138
00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:43.580
[S03] Trap three: victim mentality and the habit of blaming others.

139
00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:48.480
[S03] The eternal child syndrome can easily pull you into a victim mindset, where every outside

140
00:08:48.509 --> 00:08:51.779
[S03] pressure is seen as proof that the world is against you.

141
00:08:51.809 --> 00:08:55.919
[S03] This is like the story of Peter Pan, who always sees the adult Captain Hook as the

142
00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:57.129
[S03] evil enemy.

143
00:08:57.159 --> 00:09:01.940
[S03] In real life, Captain Hook represents your boss, the rules of society, and the ticking

144
00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:04.429
[S03] clock of time that is pushing you to grow up.

145
00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:08.220
[S03] Instead of allowing your mind to accept the reality that you need more effort and

146
00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:13.259
[S03] discipline, this syndrome tricks you into seeing all of those pressures as unfair.

147
00:09:13.289 --> 00:09:18.610
[S03] Yes, the economy can be difficult, the job market can be harsh, and society is not always

148
00:09:18.639 --> 00:09:19.580
[S03] fair.

149
00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:25.149
[S03] But the trap is this: if you use those things as the only reason not to act, you accidentally

150
00:09:25.179 --> 00:09:28.179
[S03] hand over full control of your life to your circumstances.

151
00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:33.059
[S03] This mechanism keeps you stuck in the land of avoidance, where you feel protected.

152
00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:38.149
[S03] But in reality, you are slowly losing your ability to take responsibility for your own

153
00:09:38.179 --> 00:09:39.100
[S03] life.

154
00:09:39.129 --> 00:09:39.850
[S03] But you know what?

155
00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:43.629
[S03] Recognizing these three traps is not about beating yourself up and calling yourself

156
00:09:43.659 --> 00:09:44.480
[S03] a failure.

157
00:09:44.509 --> 00:09:49.129
[S03] Recognizing them is about understanding your invisible enemy, and knowing this, you have

158
00:09:49.159 --> 00:09:51.279
[S03] the power to change it starting today.

159
00:09:51.309 --> 00:09:55.100
[S03] If you saw yourself in the parts we just went through, what you need right now is not

160
00:09:55.129 --> 00:09:56.629
[S03] to sink into guilt.

161
00:09:56.659 --> 00:10:01.230
[S03] Guilt can make you blame yourself, but it does not necessarily make you grow up.

162
00:10:01.259 --> 00:10:04.480
[S03] What you need are the real actions of an adult.

163
00:10:04.509 --> 00:10:06.940
[S03] There are four steps to step out of the cocoon.

164
00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:10.820
[S03] Step one: completely change the way you think about growing up.

165
00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:15.179
[S03] One of the biggest misunderstandings of the eternal child state is the belief that growing

166
00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:21.750
[S03] up, making money, and carrying responsibility will turn them into someone ordinary, kill

167
00:10:21.779 --> 00:10:25.950
[S03] their creativity, and erase the dreams and ambitions of their youth.

168
00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:27.879
[S03] They see adulthood as a prison.

169
00:10:27.909 --> 00:10:30.879
[S03] But the truth is, the exact opposite.

170
00:10:30.909 --> 00:10:34.860
[S03] You need to understand that responsibility and commitment are not chains.

171
00:10:34.889 --> 00:10:37.379
[S03] They are the strongest protective walls for your dreams.

172
00:10:37.409 --> 00:10:41.500
[S03] Just imagine this: if someone has a talent for writing but does not have the discipline

173
00:10:41.529 --> 00:10:45.460
[S03] to sit down at the desk every day, that talent will disappear into nothing.

174
00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:49.480
[S03] If someone has a brilliant business idea but is afraid of risk and refuses to learn

175
00:10:49.509 --> 00:10:52.779
[S03] how to manage money, that idea will stay on paper forever.

176
00:10:52.809 --> 00:10:56.059
[S03] Growing up does not kill the creative child inside you.

177
00:10:56.090 --> 00:11:01.250
[S03] It gives that child a structure, a solid foundation, so ideas can become real in the

178
00:11:01.279 --> 00:11:02.740
[S03] real world.

179
00:11:02.769 --> 00:11:06.440
[S03] Step two: bravely embrace being ordinary.

180
00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:11.190
[S03] The psychology of the eternal child often makes you look down on normal tasks and daily

181
00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.779
[S03] routines because they seem too small compared to the special image you once believed about

182
00:11:15.809 --> 00:11:16.779
[S03] yourself.

183
00:11:16.809 --> 00:11:20.139
[S03] To break this mechanism, you have to start doing those small and boring things with

184
00:11:20.169 --> 00:11:21.820
[S03] the greatest seriousness.

185
00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:26.480
[S03] Start by cleaning your own room, washing and folding your own clothes neatly, and

186
00:11:26.509 --> 00:11:31.070
[S03] try doing it in silence, without using music or your phone to pull your attention somewhere

187
00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:31.820
[S03] else.

188
00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.139
[S03] Focus completely on the action itself.

189
00:11:34.169 --> 00:11:36.820
[S03] Pay your own electric and water bills on time.

190
00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:42.250
[S03] Take a normal job, whether it is physical work or a low-paying office job, and try

191
00:11:42.279 --> 00:11:43.899
[S03] to do it as well as you can.

192
00:11:43.929 --> 00:11:48.190
[S03] When you allow yourself to do these ordinary things seriously, you are pulling your mind

193
00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.970
[S03] down from the clouds and back to the ground.

194
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:55.649
[S03] You are directly interacting with reality, and realizing that the world does not stop

195
00:11:55.679 --> 00:11:58.820
[S03] just because you are avoiding it or because you are not ready.

196
00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:01.990
[S03] Step three: practice exposure therapy with failure.

197
00:12:02.009 --> 00:12:06.090
[S03] Because the core fear of this state is the fear that you are not good enough, the way

198
00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:11.490
[S03] to overcome it is not to avoid failure forever, but to slowly expose yourself to situations

199
00:12:11.509 --> 00:12:13.509
[S03] where real failure is possible.

200
00:12:13.539 --> 00:12:17.299
[S03] Choose one thing you have been putting off for a long time simply because you were afraid

201
00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:18.830
[S03] you would not do it well.

202
00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:22.549
[S03] It might be finishing your resume and sending it to five different companies.

203
00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:26.799
[S03] It might be signing up for a new skills course, where you accept that you may be clumsy

204
00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:27.740
[S03] at the beginning.

205
00:12:54.879 --> 00:12:59.049
[S04] Step four: Find an outside structure that holds you accountable.

206
00:12:59.049 --> 00:13:03.389
[S04] One very strong tendency of the eternal child is constantly negotiating with himself

207
00:13:03.389 --> 00:13:04.879
[S04] to avoid discomfort.

208
00:13:04.879 --> 00:13:08.539
[S04] When things get hard, your mind can easily come up with hundreds of reasons that sound

209
00:13:08.539 --> 00:13:13.669
[S04] reasonable just so you can delay, rest a little longer, or crawl back into your soft

210
00:13:13.669 --> 00:13:14.850
[S04] bed with your phone.

211
00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:19.730
[S04] That is why, in the beginning, you cannot fully rely on your own willpower.

212
00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:25.370
[S04] You need to find a mentor, a teacher, a strict boss, or a community of highly disciplined

213
00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:26.059
[S04] men.

214
00:13:26.059 --> 00:13:30.440
[S04] Find people who are honest enough to point out when you are making excuses, serious enough

215
00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:35.629
[S04] not to pamper your procrastination, and demanding enough to expect you to finish what you promised.

216
00:13:35.629 --> 00:13:39.309
[S04] The pressure of a disciplined environment and the supervision of the people around

217
00:13:39.309 --> 00:13:42.720
[S04] you will be what keeps your feet on the ground whenever the child inside you wants

218
00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:44.830
[S04] to fly back into the fantasy world.

219
00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:49.990
[S04] These four steps are not easy, but they are a very practical path for you to start pulling

220
00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:51.850
[S04] yourself back into real life.

221
00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:56.029
[S04] Do not let your potential stay forever in the words "almost."

222
00:13:56.029 --> 00:14:00.960
[S04] Do not wait until the end of your life, look back, and have nothing but regret.

223
00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:04.480
[S04] The difference between a person with real achievements and a person forever trapped

224
00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:07.429
[S04] in potential is not natural talent.

225
00:14:07.429 --> 00:14:11.399
[S04] It is whether you dare to step out of the imaginary world and face the scratches of

226
00:14:11.399 --> 00:14:12.570
[S04] reality.

227
00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.460
[S04] There may be no magical moment that pulls you out of that room.

228
00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:18.870
[S04] There is also no magic that will turn you into a successful person overnight if you

229
00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:22.730
[S04] keep letting each day pass unconsciously in front of a screen.

230
00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:27.519
[S04] Maybe you have lived too long on the surface of life, inside a feeling of safety that

231
00:14:27.519 --> 00:14:31.299
[S04] seems comfortable but has been quietly trapping you.

232
00:14:31.299 --> 00:14:34.399
[S04] Now, it is time to step into the deep waters of reality.

233
00:14:34.399 --> 00:14:38.509
[S04] And before we close today's video, think carefully about a quote from Carl Jung.

234
00:14:38.509 --> 00:14:43.429
[S04] "The greatest burden a child must bear is the unlived life of its parents."

235
00:14:43.429 --> 00:14:48.980
[S04] If you keep avoiding life and living temporarily today, what you lose is not just time.

236
00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:53.799
[S04] Little by little, that helplessness and regret can accidentally become something you carry

237
00:14:53.799 --> 00:14:58.509
[S04] into love, family, friendship, and even the way you treat the next generation.
